  "worked_solution": "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)-9({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\pi }{{n}^{3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+9)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\pi }{{n}^{3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{a}_{n}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+9)=\\frac{\\pi }{{n}^{3}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{\\pi }{{n}^{3}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+9)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{{n}^{3}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+9)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$",
